<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chad Gadya</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">early version c. 13th–14th century; printed in Haggadahs in Prague, <a href="/years/1527">1527</a> and <a href="/years/1590">1590</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as חד גדיא</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chad">Chad</a> Gadya (&quot;one little goat&quot; or &quot;one kid&quot;) is a playful cumulative song sung at the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/passover">Passover</a> Seder, the ritual feast that opens the Jewish holiday of Passover. An early version appears in a prayer book of the Jewish community of Provence from about the 13th or 14th century, and the song was printed in Haggadahs in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a> in 1527 and 1590, which makes it a late addition to the Seder.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Chad Gadya</b> (&quot;one little goat&quot; or &quot;one kid&quot;) is a playful cumulative song sung at the end of the Passover Seder, the ritual feast that opens the Jewish holiday of Passover.</blockquote><a href="/songs/chad-gadya">Chad Gadya</a></li></ul></section>
